<chapter xml:id="chapter3-4" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Section 3.4: Other Statistics</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>So far you have compared two groups by examining the difference in the conditional proportions. But that is not the only choice of statistic, and not always the most informative one, especially when the outcomes of interest are rare. In this section you will explore two alternative statistics for comparing two groups, the relative risk and the odds ratio, and how to draw inferences with them, before putting the chapter's ideas together in a final application.</p>

    <p><xref ref="inv-3-8" text="custom">Investigation 3.8: Peanut Allergies</xref> <ndash/> Relative risk</p>
    <p><xref ref="inv-3-9" text="custom">Investigation 3.9: Smoking and Lung Cancer</xref> <ndash/> Types of observational studies, Odds ratios</p>
    <p><xref ref="inv-3-10" text="custom">Investigation 3.10: Sleepy Drivers</xref> <ndash/> Application</p>
  </introduction>

